    "content": "Monstera Deliciosa (Monstera deliciosa) — Houseplant, Easy to grow. The split-leaf classic. Fast, forgiving and dramatic once it starts fenestrating — it just needs bright indirect light and a chunky, free-draining mix.\nLight: Bright indirect light. Direct midday sun scorches leaves; deep shade stops fenestration.\nWater: Water when the top 3-5 cm of mix is dry — roughly every 7-10 days in summer, every 2-3 weeks in winter.\nSoil: Chunky aroid mix: 40% potting soil, 30% orchid bark, 20% perlite, 10% coco coir.\nHumidity: Happy at 50-60% RH; higher humidity gives larger leaves and more splits.\nTemperature: 18-30°C. Keep above 12°C and away from cold draughts.\nFeeding: Balanced liquid feed at half strength every 2-4 weeks in spring and summer; none in winter.\nPropagation: Cut below a node with an aerial root, root in water for 3-4 weeks, then pot up.\nToxicity: Toxic to cats, dogs and children if chewed (calcium oxalate crystals).\nCommon problems: No splits in leaves — cause: Too little light, or plant still juvenile; fix: Move closer to a bright window and give it a moss pole to climb. | Yellow lower leaves — cause: Overwatering or compacted soil; fix: Let the mix dry further, check drainage, repot into a chunkier mix. | Brown crispy edges — cause: Low humidity or underwatering; fix: Group plants together, raise humidity, water more consistently.\nTips: Give it something to climb — vertical support triggers bigger, more split leaves. Wipe leaves monthly; dust cuts light absorption noticeably."
